<http://data.silknow.org/object/97cdad17-4f70-33a5-8f07-47438c6587be>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Pieces from a Floorspread:   The rows of single flowers are typical of 'Classic' Mughal design, and can be seen  on objects of all materials.  The deep red of the ground changes colours with the direction of the velvet pile, so that although these two pieces are dyed the same colours, one appears much lighter than the other. [1994]"@en , "These large fragments of finely woven silk velvet would have been part of a large floor-covering. The deep red field is achieved by dyeing with the expensive insect dye called lac.The rows of single flowers are typical of 'classic' Mughal design, which was used in objects in all media from the mid-17th century onwards. The deep red of the ground appears to change colours with the direction of the velvet pile, so that although these two pieces are dyed the same colours, one appears much lighter than the other."@en , "Pieces of a velvet floorspread, Mughal, late 17th century"@en , "Pieces from a velvet floorspread. Deep, lac-dyed red field with staggered rows of standing flowering plants."@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/683168ad-912f-389d-b066-b9e4ad546351> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/d26239e2-c908-31a7-9f02-1de56f4bcfcf> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/9a6c03ed-73a7-31a3-8478-7546dab6c389> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/e28cd444-f911-3cb9-a3b6-fb6f99745eeb> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/b18feae2-b244-3620-97b3-782bf801432b> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/925231e1-fa3f-33ea-aeb3-f8196a39fba3> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/f0ada2ff-fceb-37a9-8d0c-7d84077be0af> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/67baa5a8-cbb4-3432-ab82-839d1ae758a4> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/6398243d-9c28-356a-8c00-069fbea2a91b> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "Pieces from a Floorspread:   The rows of single flowers are typical of 'Classic' Mughal design, and can be seen  on objects of all materials.  The deep red of the ground changes colours with the direction of the velvet pile, so that although these two pieces are dyed the same colours, one appears much lighter than the other. [1994]"@en , "These large fragments of finely woven silk velvet would have been part of a large floor-covering. The deep red field is achieved by dyeing with the expensive insect dye called lac.The rows of single flowers are typical of 'classic' Mughal design, which was used in objects in all media from the mid-17th century onwards. The deep red of the ground appears to change colours with the direction of the velvet pile, so that although these two pieces are dyed the same colours, one appears much lighter than the other."@en , "Pieces from a velvet floorspread. Deep, lac-dyed red field with staggered rows of standing flowering plants."@en , "Pieces of a velvet floorspread, Mughal, late 17th century"@en ;
        ecrm:P43_has_dimension         <http://data.silknow.org/object/97cdad17-4f70-33a5-8f07-47438c6587be/dimension/1> , <http://data.silknow.org/object/97cdad17-4f70-33a5-8f07-47438c6587be/dimension/2> ;
